    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0046",
      "sequence": 58,
      "meaning": "Reflecting thus, I reveal my own delusion; Master, show tenderness to your servant and remove it. The name Rāma has immeasurable power, sung by saints, Purāṇas and Upaniṣads. Imperishable Śambhu—Lord Śiva, treasury of knowledge and virtue—repeats it constantly. Creatures of all four modes of birth who die in Kāśī attain the supreme state, for compassionate Śiva imparts the teaching through Rāma’s greatness. Which Rāma is this, Lord? I ask you; treasury of compassion, explain so I may understand. One Rāma is the prince and king of Ayodhyā, whose deeds are known throughout the world. He suffered immeasurable grief in separation from his wife, then grew wrathful and killed Rāvaṇa in battle. Is that Rāma the Lord whom Tripurāri repeats, or is it someone else? You are the abode of truth and all-knowing; reflect with discernment and tell me.",
