    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0047",
      "sequence": 59,
      "meaning": "Master, tell that story fully so my heavy delusion may disappear. Yājñavalkya smiled and said, ‘Raghupati’s lordship is known to you. Your mind, action and speech are immersed in Rāma; I understand your clever purpose. You wish to hear Rāma’s profound virtues and have asked as though exceedingly foolish. Dear one, listen reverently with attentive mind while I tell Rāma’s lovely story. Great delusion is a huge buffalo-demon, and Rāma’s story the terrible goddess Kālī. Rāma’s story resembles moonbeams, which saintly cātakas drink. Bhavānī once raised this same doubt, and Mahādeva then explained the answer. According to my understanding, I now tell the dialogue of Umā and Śambhu; hear, sage, the time and occasion from which it arose, and your sorrow will disappear.’",
