    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0048",
      "sequence": 60,
      "meaning": "Once in the Tretā age Śambhu went to the sage Agastya, accompanied by Satī, Bhavānī, mother of the world. The sage worshipped him, recognizing the Lord of all. The eminent sage narrated Rāma’s story; Maheśa heard it and felt supreme joy. The sage asked about beautiful devotion to Hari, and Śambhu, finding him qualified, explained it. Speaking and hearing the account of Raghupati’s virtues, the Lord of the Mountain remained there several days. Then Tripurāri took leave of the sage and set out for home with Dakṣa’s daughter. At that time, to remove Earth’s burden, Hari had taken incarnation in Raghu’s line. Obeying his father, the imperishable Lord had renounced his kingdom and wandered the Daṇḍaka forest as an ascetic. As Hara travelled he reflected in his heart: how might he obtain a sight of the Lord? Yet if he went, everyone would know that the Lord had descended in a concealed form. Great agitation filled Śaṅkara’s heart, while Satī did not know its secret. Says Tulsī: the mind longed for the sight, yet felt fear, while the eyes were greedy.",
