    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0049",
      "sequence": 61,
      "meaning": "The Lord had accepted that Rāvaṇa should seek death at a human’s hand and wished to make Brahmā’s word true. ‘If I do not go, regret will remain;’ thus Īśa reflected, unable to resolve the situation. At that time ten-headed Rāvaṇa went and took the base Mārīca with him; Mārīca instantly became the deceptive deer. Through deceit the fool carried off Vaidehī, not knowing the Lord’s power as it truly was. Having killed the deer, Hari returned with his brother; seeing the empty hermitage, tears filled his eyes. Distraught by separation like a man, Raghurāī wandered the forest searching with his brother. The one from whom union and separation are never absent visibly displayed the sorrow of separation. Raghupati’s deeds are exceedingly wondrous and known to the supremely wise; the dull-witted, overcome by delusion, hold something else in their hearts.",
