    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0050",
      "sequence": 62,
      "meaning": "At that time Śambhu saw Rāma and extraordinary joy arose in his heart. Filling his eyes, he beheld the ocean of beauty; recognizing the inopportune moment, he did not introduce himself. Saying, ‘Victory to being-consciousness-bliss, purifier of the world,’ the destroyer of Kāma went on. Śiva continued with Satī, repeatedly thrilled in devotion, an abode of compassion. Satī saw that condition of Śambhu and a profound doubt arose within her. ‘Śaṅkara is Lord of the world, worshipped by the world; gods, humans and sages all bow their heads to him. Yet he bowed to a king’s son, calling him being-consciousness-bliss and the supreme abode. He became absorbed on beholding that prince’s beauty, and even now the love in his heart cannot be restrained. Can the all-pervading, stainless, unborn, partless, desireless and nondual Brahman assume a body and become a man—the one whom even the Veda does not know?’",
